window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"][9] = "<p><strong>Data.</strong> Of 100 new teams (invented): 70 create a first event, 45 invite at least one colleague, and 24 get at least two colleagues to accept within seven days. Thirty-day retention is 62% for the 24 teams with two or more acceptances and 18% for the other 76 teams. Overall: 0.24 × 62% + 0.76 × 18% = 14.9% + 13.7% = 28.6%, about 29%.</p>\n<p><strong>Problem definition.</strong> Which early behavior signals that a team has found value, and does causing more teams to do it raise retention?</p>\n<p><strong>Analysis.</strong> The funnel loses teams at every step: 30 before a first event (100 to 70), 25 before an invite (70 to 45) and 21 before two acceptances (45 to 24). The steepest relative loss, 47%, is between inviting and getting two colleagues to accept. The candidate activation event is &quot;two colleagues accept within seven days.&quot; The retention gap (62% versus 18%) is large, so the event deserves attention. But the gap is a correlation. Teams that invite and get acceptances are probably teams that had a real shared need, and they would have stayed anyway (self-selection). If the gap were fully causal and a nudge raised the share reaching the event from 24% to 29%, overall retention would rise to 0.29 × 62% + 0.71 × 18% = 18.0% + 12.8% = 30.8%, about 31%, a gain of about 2.2 points. An experiment (C14) can measure how much of that gain is real.</p>\n<p><strong>Options.</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Option</th>\n<th>Optimizes</th>\n<th>Costs</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A. Declare &quot;two acceptances&quot; the activation metric and build around it</td>\n<td>Focus</td>\n<td>If the gap is mostly self-selection, effort is misdirected</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B. Run an experiment: a nudge that prompts invitations, with the activation share and 30-day retention as metrics</td>\n<td>Evidence on cause</td>\n<td>Four weeks</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C. Make no change until a larger study is done</td>\n<td>No wasted effort</td>\n<td>Delays learning</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> B costs a month and tells the team how much of the correlation is real. A is faster and risks building on a mirage.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision.</strong> Run B. Define activation as two accepted invitations within seven days, track its share and time to reach it, and add a counter metric: complaints about invitation emails, to catch a nudge that spams.</p>\n<p><strong>Outcome.</strong> In this fictional scenario the nudge raised the share reaching activation from 24% to 29% and raised overall 30-day retention from 29% to 30% (invented), about one point instead of the 2.2 points the correlation implied. About 45% of the apparent effect was real (1.0 ÷ 2.2), and the rest was self-selection. Complaints about invitations did not rise.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative outcomes.</strong> If retention had risen by the full 2.2 points, the correlation would have been largely causal, and the team would have invested in invitations. If retention had not moved, the team would have looked for another lever (shared calendars, integrations). In every case the lesson is the method: find the candidate from the data, then test whether it is a cause.</p>\n<span id=\"cs-c05-02\"></span>\n<h5>CS-C05-02 · Facebook, 2018: choosing to lower time spent · Verified · Advanced</h5>\n<p><em>Sources (types): Facebook's News Feed announcement of 11 January 2018 (&quot;News Feed FYI: Bringing People Closer Together,&quot; by Adam Mosseri, on the company's newsroom); its fourth-quarter 2017 results press release (filed with the US Securities and Exchange Commission) and the transcript of the earnings call of 31 January 2018 (primary); and CNBC, Fortune and TechCrunch reports of the results. The analysis is the author's. Re-read in October 2026.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Situation.</strong> On 11 January 2018, Facebook announced a change to News Feed: its head of News Feed said the goal given to product teams was changing from helping people find relevant content to helping them have more meaningful social interactions, so that people would see less public content, such as posts from businesses, brands and media (including videos), and more posts that spark conversation between people <code>[V: Facebook newsroom, 11 January 2018]</code>. In its fourth-quarter 2017 results on January 31, 2018, the company said that changes it had already made, such as showing fewer viral videos, reduced time spent on Facebook by roughly 5%, about 50 million hours a day <code>[V: Q4 2017 press release and call]</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> A company whose revenue came almost entirely from advertising, where time spent had been a core engagement measure. The announcement came as the company faced public scrutiny of its effect on society, and as daily active users in the US and Canada fell slightly in the quarter, from about 185 million to 184 million, the first quarterly decline the company had reported in any market, and the chief financial officer said the changes contributed to a decline of 700,000 compared with the previous quarter <code>[V: earnings call; CNBC and TechCrunch reports]</code>. Global daily active users still grew to 1.40 billion, up 14% on a year earlier, but quarter-over-quarter growth of about 2% was the slowest the company had reported, according to TechCrunch.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> The company's statements: it estimated the changes cut time spent by about 5% (about 50 million hours a day); the CEO said on the call that helping people connect is more important than maximizing the time they spend on Facebook, and that he expected time spent and some measures of engagement to go down <code>[V: earnings call, 31 January 2018]</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Problem definition.</strong> Which measure should a social product try to raise: total time spent, which is easy to measure and was growing, or a measure of how much of that time is connected to people and valued by them, which is harder to define?</p>\n<p><strong>Analysis.</strong> This is a documented case of choosing to move a headline metric down. Three points apply to metrics. First, time spent is a proxy (<a href=\"#gl-proxy-metric\">proxy metric</a>) for value, and it can rise through content that does not benefit users; the company's own statement acknowledged that passive consumption differs from interaction. Second, the company paired the decision with a different measure (meaningful interactions), which is harder to define and measure, and which raises the risk of the new measure being gamed. Third, the company said openly that the decision would reduce the old measure, which prepared investors and employees for the change. Whether the change improved well-being, and whether the long-term business effect was positive, are separate questions that this case does not answer; accounts differ, and the data needed is not public.</p>\n<p><strong>Options.</strong> <em>The author's reconstruction.</em></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Option</th>\n<th>Optimizes</th>\n<th>Costs</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A. Keep optimizing time spent</td>\n<td>A well-understood metric and short-term revenue</td>\n<td>Risk that value per hour falls and trust erodes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B. Shift ranking toward meaningful interactions and accept lower time spent</td>\n<td>Value per user and long-term trust, as the company argued</td>\n<td>A lower headline metric, investor reaction, a harder metric to define</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C. Shift gradually and report both</td>\n<td>Smoother transition</td>\n<td>Slower, mixed signals</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A near-term headline metric against a long-term, less measurable goal. The company chose B and accepted that the metric would fall.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision.</strong> As documented, the company changed News Feed ranking and reported the decline in time spent.</p>\n<p><strong>Outcome.</strong> Documented: a stated reduction of about 5% in time spent, and statements from executives that they did not expect the changes to drag down ad revenue <code>[V: Fortune, 31 January 2018]</code>. What happened afterward to well-being and to the business is outside this case.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative outcomes.</strong> <em>Inference.</em> If the company had kept time spent as the target, short-term numbers might have continued to rise, with the risks of lower trust and regulatory attention that the company described. If it had defined meaningful interactions poorly, the new target could itself have been gamed. A counter metric for the new target (for example, the share of interactions that users later report as unwanted) would have exposed that.</p>\n<p><strong>Lesson for an interview answer.</strong> A metric is a choice about what to value. When a headline number is rising for reasons that do not serve users, say so, name a better measure, name its own gaming risk, and say how you would prepare stakeholders for the number falling.</p>\n<span id=\"c05-11-interview-questions-and-model-answers\"></span>\n<h3>11. Interview questions and model answers</h3>\n<p>Eight questions: two Beginner, three Intermediate, three Hard. Model answers are <code>Illustrative</code>; every figure is an assumption. Each is written against the five behaviors in section 1.</p>\n<span id=\"iq-c05-01\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C05-01 · Beginner · metrics · AN</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> What would you use as the success metric for a &quot;save for later&quot; feature in a news app?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Number of articles saved,&quot; with no link to value.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Starts from the goal, chooses a reading-based primary metric with a definition, and adds a guardrail.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Explains why saves alone are gameable, defines the denominator, adds a counter metric for unread clutter, and says how to learn whether the feature causes more reading.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> The goal of save-for-later is that people read things they cared about but had no time for. The behavior that shows value is reading saved articles, not saving them, because saving costs a tap and does not mean anything was gained.</p>\n<p>So my primary metric is the saved-article read rate: of articles saved in a week, the share opened and read to at least 50% within seven days of saving. The numerator is saved articles read to 50% within seven days. The denominator is articles saved that week, excluding saves made by import or by a bulk tool. The unit is the saved article, and I'd report it by user as well, since a few heavy savers can dominate.</p>\n<p>Inputs: the share of weekly active readers who save at least one article, saves per saver, and the share of saved articles that get a reminder or reappear in a list. Guardrails: total reading time must not fall, which would suggest saving replaces reading now with reading never; and article load time must stay flat. My counter metric is the share of saved articles never opened after 30 days. If it climbs, the feature is a graveyard.</p>\n<p>Target: a read rate of 35% within eight weeks (assumed). To learn whether the feature causes more reading, I'd compare readers who see the save button with a holdout who do not, looking at articles read per week. I'd change my mind if saves rise and reads do not: the metric would show the feature is a habit of hoarding, and the fix would be reminders or surfacing saves at a better time.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> The saved-article read rate is 18%. Is that bad? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I cannot say without a comparison, so I would build two. First, the same rate over time by weekly cohort: is it rising or falling? Second, the read-to-50% rate for articles people open directly, which might be about 55%. If saved articles are read at 18% against 55% for direct opens, four in five saves are never read, which suggests saving has become a way of feeling organized. I would also split by user: a few heavy savers can pull the average down. The fix to test is a reminder or a short reading list, with the size of the unread backlog as the counter metric.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How would you learn whether the feature causes more reading, not just more saving? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;With a holdout. Hide the feature from a random 5% of weekly active readers for four weeks and compare articles read to 50% per reader, including both saved and unsaved articles. If readers with the feature read more in total, it adds reading; if they only shift reading from unsaved to saved articles, it adds nothing. I would read the effect by week, since a novelty bump can fade, and watch the share of saved articles that are never opened as a guardrail.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c05-02\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C05-02 · Beginner · metrics · AN</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> What is the difference between a leading and a lagging indicator? Give a product example and a way it can go wrong.</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> Defines both loosely without a product example.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Gives clear definitions, an example pair, and why a team needs both.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Explains the need to validate the leading indicator's link to the outcome, gives a failure mode (a leading indicator that stops predicting, or is gamed), and says how to check.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> A lagging indicator reports a result after it has happened: quarterly churn, annual revenue, 90-day retention. It is reliable and slow, so by the time it moves it is hard to change. A leading indicator moves earlier and predicts the result: how many days a new user was active in the first week.</p>\n<p>A product pair: 90-day retention is the lagging indicator for a learning app, and the share of new users who complete three lessons in their first week is the leading indicator. The team can see the leading indicator in seven days and act on it, while the lagging one takes three months. A team needs both: the lagging indicator says whether we succeeded and the leading one says whether we are on track in time to change course.</p>\n<p>How it goes wrong. First, the link may not be real. If users who complete three lessons are simply the ones who would have stayed anyway, pushing more users to do it may not raise retention. I'd check by correlation over past cohorts and then by an experiment that increases first-week lessons and watches 90-day retention. Second, the leading indicator becomes a target and is gamed: making the first three lessons very short raises the share completing them and teaches nothing. A counter metric such as a short quiz on what was taught would show it. Third, the relationship drifts as the product or audience changes, so I'd recheck it each quarter.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you check that a leading indicator really predicts the lagging one? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;First look at history: among past cohorts, compare 90-day retention for users who completed three lessons in week one with those who did not, say 62% against 21%. That shows association. Then test whether it is a lever: run an experiment that raises week-one completion with a nudge, and see whether 90-day retention moves for the nudged group. If completion rises and retention does not, the indicator is a marker of motivated users, not something the team can pull. I would repeat the check each quarter because the link can weaken.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How can the team make the leading indicator stop working once it is a target? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;By optimizing the number instead of the behavior. If the target is three lessons in week one, the team can make the first lessons shorter or easier, completion rises, and retention stays flat because the early lessons no longer signal commitment. The defense is a guardrail on the quality of the behavior, such as quiz scores or lesson difficulty, and a standing review of the relationship between the indicator and the outcome. If the correlation falls, retire the indicator.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c05-03\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C05-03 · Intermediate · metrics · AN · Apple</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Define success metrics for a smartwatch sleep-tracking feature, including a privacy guardrail.</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Number of people who use it and their sleep scores,&quot; with no guardrails.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Sets a goal, a primary usage metric, inputs, product guardrails (battery, accuracy) and a privacy guardrail.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Treats consent and on-device processing as part of the metric design, accounts for the bias from users who opt out of measurement, adds a counter metric for harm (anxiety about sleep scores), and avoids health claims it cannot support.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> <em>Setting only: no company data is used or implied.</em> The goal is that people understand their sleep and can choose to act on it, which requires that they wear the watch to bed regularly and look at the results. The feature is not a medical product in this answer, so I'd measure usage and perceived usefulness, not health outcomes I cannot validate.</p>\n<p>Primary metric: sustained overnight use, defined as the share of eligible watch owners who wear the watch to sleep on at least four nights a week for four consecutive weeks. Numerator: owners meeting that rule in a four-week window. Denominator: owners with the feature available and the sleep setting turned on at the start of the window. Inputs: setup completion, nights worn per week, and morning review (the share of worn nights followed by opening the summary).</p>\n<p>Product guardrails: battery life through a full day after a night of tracking must not fall below the current standard, since people who cannot charge in the morning will drop the feature, and the rate of nights with no data due to wear or charging problems should not rise. Counter metrics: the share of users who turn tracking off within 30 days (to see if it annoys), and the rate of reports or survey responses from people who say that sleep scores make them anxious about sleep, because a score can create the problem it measures.</p>\n<p>Privacy guardrail: sleep data is sensitive personal data. Where possible I'd compute usage metrics on the device or in aggregate, report only counts and rates, and collect nothing at the individual level without consent. The guardrail metrics are the opt-in rate for sharing analytics and a count of data-access incidents, which must be zero. The cost is measurement bias: the data only covers people who opted in, who may be more engaged than those who did not. I'd say that in the result, and I'd supplement with an in-product survey that does not depend on tracking, to see whether opt-in users differ.</p>\n<p>I'd change my plan if the nights-worn distribution shows a bimodal pattern: some people wear it nightly and many never, which would point to setup or comfort and not to the feature itself.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you handle the bias from owners who opt out of sleep measurement? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I treat the opt-in rate as a metric of its own and I do not extrapolate. People who turn sleep tracking on differ from those who do not, perhaps in age, health interest or comfort with data, so any result describes the opted-in group. I would report the share of eligible owners who opt in, compare the two groups on observable traits such as device age and general activity, and use a randomized prompt or a holdout of who is offered the feature when I need a causal estimate. In the write-up I would say plainly that the findings apply to opted-in owners.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What counter metric would you add for harm, such as anxiety about sleep scores? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Two. A behavioral one: the share of users who turn the feature off within a week of seeing a low score, which suggests the score upset them. And a survey item on a small sample, asking whether checking the score makes them feel better or worse about their sleep. I would set a threshold in advance, for example that disable-after-low-score must not exceed 5%, and review support contacts. I would also keep the wording of scores neutral and avoid health claims the product cannot support.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c05-04\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C05-04 · Intermediate · metrics · AN · Netflix</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> How would you measure the success of a personalized recommendations row on a streaming service?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Click-through rate and hours watched.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Defines satisfying viewing as the goal, uses a funnel from impression to sustained viewing, and adds guardrails.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Warns that click and hours are gameable, adds a regret counter metric, considers diversity and long-term retention, and proposes a long-run holdout.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> <em>Setting only: no company data or internal metrics are claimed.</em> The goal of the row is to help a member find something they want to watch quickly and enjoy it, so that they keep subscribing. A click is not the goal: a misleading thumbnail raises clicks and lowers satisfaction.</p>\n<p>The funnel: impression of the row, play started from the row, and sustained viewing (watching at least 10 minutes, or finishing the title). Primary metric: satisfied plays per active member per week, defined as plays started from the row that reach 10 minutes, divided by active members in the week, excluding autoplay previews. The 10-minute threshold filters accidental starts, and it can be tuned by title length.</p>\n<p>Inputs: the row's play rate (plays per impression), the sustained-view rate (plays reaching 10 minutes), and time from opening the service to a satisfied play. Guardrails: total viewing per member should not fall (which would mean the row replaces better choices), and the diversity of titles played (share of plays outside the most popular ten percent of titles) should not collapse, so the row does not narrow taste. Counter metric: regret, defined as plays abandoned within two minutes, which a clickbait-style row would raise.</p>\n<p>The long-run measure is retention. Satisfied plays may lead retention by weeks or months, so I'd run a long-term holdout of members who see a simpler row, and compare their cancellation rate. Short tests would see plays, and only a holdout would see whether the row helps keep members. I'd change my view if satisfied plays rose and retention did not, which would mean the row shifts viewing within the service but does not add value.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How would you detect that satisfied plays are being inflated by misleading thumbnails or autoplay? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I would look at what happens after the click. A misleading thumbnail produces plays that end quickly, so I would compare the share of plays abandoned before 10 minutes across thumbnail variants and across rows, and I would exclude autoplay previews from the metric as the definition says. A regret metric helps: the share of plays from the row that are abandoned within two minutes and followed by another search. If a variant raises plays but also raises early abandonment, it is gaming the click, and I would judge it on sustained viewing and long-run retention.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> Why a long-run holdout, and for how long? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Short-term plays can rise while long-term satisfaction falls, for example if the row narrows what people see. A small holdout of about 1% of members who see the old row, kept for around three months, lets me compare retention, variety of titles watched and cancellations, which short tests cannot show. The cost is that 1% of members get a possibly worse experience, which I would accept for a bounded time and stop early if the holdout showed clear harm.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c05-05\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C05-05 · Intermediate · metrics · AN</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> What metrics would you use to evaluate an AI writing assistant that suggests text as people type?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Number of users and number of suggestions.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Uses acceptance and edit behavior as the primary signal, adds quality, latency and cost guardrails.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Notes that acceptance can mean trust or inattention, adds an audit of accepted suggestions, links cost to acceptance, and separates offline quality evaluation from online metrics.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> The goal is that people finish writing faster or better with suggestions they trust. The primary signal is suggestions accepted and kept: of suggestions shown, the share accepted and not substantially edited or deleted within a minute (the numerator is accepted suggestions that survive; the denominator is suggestions shown; the unit is the suggestion, reported also per user).</p>\n<p>Inputs: how often suggestions are shown relative to typing, acceptance rate, edit distance after acceptance, and the share of documents completed with the assistant on. Guardrails: latency of suggestions (the 95th percentile must be short enough to arrive before the user types past it), harmful or inappropriate suggestion rate, and cost per suggestion. A counter metric for over-acceptance: acceptance can rise when users stop reading carefully, so I'd audit a sample of accepted suggestions for errors and track the rate of later corrections.</p>\n<p>Cost links to the metric: if each suggestion costs about $0.004 and the acceptance rate is 25%, the cost per accepted suggestion is $0.004 ÷ 0.25 = $0.016. Raising the acceptance rate to 40% lowers it to $0.01, so quality improvements also lower the cost per accepted suggestion.</p>\n<p>Online metrics sit on top of an offline evaluation: before launch I'd score suggestions against a held-out set with human review, so I know quality before users see it (evals are covered in C24 and C25). I'd change my plan if acceptance is high but the audit finds frequent errors: the assistant would be trusted more than it deserves.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you tell whether acceptance reflects trust or inattention? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I would audit rather than assume. Sample accepted suggestions and have reviewers grade them for correctness, and measure the share that users later edit or delete. I would also look at timing: acceptances within a fraction of a second suggest the user did not read the suggestion. A sharper test is to seed a small share of deliberately flawed suggestions in an offline evaluation and measure how often they are accepted. If flawed ones are accepted at a high rate, acceptance is not a sign of trust, and I would not use it as the headline.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How would you link cost to acceptance? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Through cost per accepted suggestion. If each suggestion shown costs $0.002 to generate and 20% are accepted and kept, the cost per accepted suggestion is $0.01. If acceptance falls to 10%, it doubles to $0.02. That tells me to show suggestions only when the predicted chance of acceptance is high enough, which saves cost and reduces noise for the user. I would set a cost budget per active user and watch it beside acceptance, so that a quality change that raises both cost and acceptance can be judged on the ratio.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c05-06\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C05-06 · Hard · metrics · AN · Meta</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Time spent per user on your social app is up 8% after a ranking change. Is that good?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Yes, more time spent is more engagement.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Asks what changed, decomposes the increase, checks quality and counter metrics, and gives a conditional answer.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Challenges time spent as a proxy for value, separates active from passive time, checks retention and well-being signals, considers the second-order effects, and says what would make it good.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> <em>Setting only: no company data is used.</em> I'd say &quot;it depends on where the time comes from,&quot; and then find out.</p>\n<p>First, clarify the metric: is it total minutes per daily user, and how is time measured (foreground time, or including time with the app idle)? What exactly changed in ranking, and when?</p>\n<p>Second, decompose. Time per user = sessions per user × minutes per session. And split the time by kind: interacting with people (commenting, messaging) versus passive consumption (scrolling, watching). If the 8% is almost all passive video, it is a different story from an 8% rise in conversations.</p>\n<p>Third, check quality. Look at next-day and 30-day retention of the exposed group compared with a holdout; at reports and &quot;not interested&quot; actions; at notification opt-outs; and at survey measures of whether people feel their time was well spent. If retention is flat or lower and negative feedback is up, the time is a short-term effect.</p>\n<p>Fourth, the second-order effects. Rankings that raise time spent often favor sensational content, and over time users who feel worse leave or reduce use. Advertisers may buy fewer impressions of poor quality.</p>\n<p>So: good if the extra time is in meaningful interaction, retention and satisfaction hold, and negative feedback does not rise; a warning if it comes from passive, high-arousal content. As a documented example of a company choosing to move this metric down on purpose, CS-C05-02 describes Facebook's 2018 decision. I'd propose making &quot;time spent in interactions that users later say they valued&quot; the primary measure for this change, with time spent as an input, and I'd run the test with a long-term holdout. I'd change my view if the holdout shows higher 90-day retention for the exposed group.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> The 8% is mostly passive video. What do you do? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I would treat it as engagement without proven value and check what it costs. I would compare next-day and 30-day retention and a short well-being survey for the exposed group against a holdout, and look at active interactions such as messages and comments. If retention is flat or lower and active interactions are down, the extra time is passive consumption, and I would ask the ranking team to rebalance toward interactions. My decision rule would be to ship only if 30-day retention is at least equal to the holdout and active interactions are not lower, whatever happens to total time.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How would you test the long-term effect of the ranking change? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;With a holdout kept long enough to see slow effects: about 2% of users on the old ranking for at least eight weeks. I would read the effect by week, since a novelty bump in the first week can mislead, and compare retention, sessions per user, active interactions and survey satisfaction. I would also check for effects on creators, such as reach for smaller accounts, because a change can raise time for viewers and hurt the supply side that keeps the content coming.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c05-07\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C05-07 · Hard · metrics · AN</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Choose a north star metric for a two-sided marketplace that connects freelancers with clients, and defend it.</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Gross revenue&quot; or &quot;number of users.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Names candidates, tests them against criteria, chooses one with a definition, and adds inputs and guardrails for both sides.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Addresses quality and liquidity, shows why the obvious candidates fail, defines the metric precisely, handles the two sides separately, and says what would change the choice as the marketplace matures.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> The value of this marketplace is a good match: a client gets work done well and a freelancer gets paid for it. Candidates:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Gross merchandise value (total paid): fails the value test because it rises with higher prices or longer projects, and fails the gaming test.</li>\n<li>Number of registered users: a vanity metric that rises by construction.</li>\n<li>Contracts started per week: better, but a contract that ends badly counts as a success.</li>\n<li>Successful contracts per week: contracts that were completed and rated satisfactory by both sides, counted in the week they completed.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>I choose the fourth. Definition: a contract is successful if the work was delivered, the client released payment without a dispute, and the client rated it at least four of five (an assumed threshold). The numerator is successful contracts completed in the week, and the unit is the contract. It passes the tests: it reflects value for both sides, the team can move it through matching and trust features, it moves in weeks, and it is hard to inflate without genuine completion.</p>\n<p>Inputs, by side: demand (clients posting jobs, jobs receiving qualified proposals, time to first proposal), supply (freelancers active, proposals per job), and match quality (share of jobs filled, hire rate). Guardrails: dispute rate, freelancer earnings per hour, client repeat rate, and the share of payments made through the platform (to detect leakage). Counter metric: the rate of jobs filled by a freelancer with very few ratings, which a push for fill rate might raise at the expense of quality.</p>\n<p>I'd revisit as the marketplace matures: when most jobs are filled, the constraint shifts from liquidity to quality, and a measure of repeat contracts between the same pair would carry more weight. I'd also watch for a gap between sides: a metric that rises because clients are well served while freelancers earn less would be a warning.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> Would one north star work for both sides, and what do you do about each side? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;One joint metric is right because the value is the match, but each side needs its own health metrics so that I can diagnose a problem. For clients I would track the share who hire again within 60 days and the time to a first accepted proposal; for freelancers, earnings per active freelancer and the share of active freelancers who win at least one contract a month. I would watch the balance by category: if one side is short, the north star will stall, and the side metrics will show which side to fix.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What would make you change the north star as the marketplace matures? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;When most successful contracts come from repeat clients, a first-success metric stops telling me much, and I would shift toward client retention and the net revenue retained from existing clients. Early on, I would use the first successful contract, because the cold start is the constraint. I would also check that the metric cannot be gamed, for example by splitting one project into several small contracts to raise the count, and add a guardrail on contract size and a quality review if it appears.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c05-08\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C05-08 · Hard · metrics · AN</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> A support team's quarterly goal is &quot;tickets closed.&quot; You suspect the number is being gamed. What do you do?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Tell the team to stop gaming and be honest.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Looks for evidence of gaming, proposes counter metrics, and redefines the goal.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Treats gaming as a system response not a character flaw, proposes a metric set that makes the cheap path visible, handles the change with the team, and states what outcome the team should actually be measured on.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> I'd start from the assumption that people are responding sensibly to what they are measured on, so the question is what the metric rewards.</p>\n<p>First, test the suspicion with data, not accusation. Look for signs: closure times that cluster just before a deadline; tickets closed and reopened; a rise in the share of tickets closed as &quot;resolved&quot; with no reply; a growing number of repeat contacts about the same issue; closure counts rising while customer satisfaction is flat or falling. If I see repeat contact up and satisfaction down, gaming is likely.</p>\n<p>Second, find what &quot;tickets closed&quot; rewards. It rewards closing easy tickets first and closing hard ones quickly, whether or not they were resolved. A <a href=\"#gl-second-order-effect\">second-order effect</a> is that hard tickets age and customers contact again.</p>\n<p>Third, redesign the metric set. The goal should be customers whose problem is solved. A primary metric could be the resolution rate: tickets resolved without a repeat contact within seven days, divided by tickets closed. Counters: customer satisfaction on resolved tickets, and the age of the oldest open tickets (to stop easy-first cherry-picking). Keep closed tickets as an input (volume), not the target.</p>\n<p>Fourth, handle it with the team. I'd show them the data about repeat contacts, explain that the old target made the bad behavior rational, and agree the new set with their input. I'd also audit a sample of closures by hand for the first two months, and I'd avoid punishing the old behavior, since the old target created it.</p>\n<p>I'd change my approach if the review shows no gaming: then the question is whether closed tickets is the right measure anyway, and the answer is probably still to add a quality counter.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you confirm gaming without accusing the team? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;By looking at aggregate patterns and sharing them as a question about the system. I would check closure spikes before deadlines, the reopen rate (for example up from 4% to 12%), repeat contacts about the same issue and customer satisfaction. I would show the team the chart and ask what the metric makes easy and what it makes hard, because people usually describe the incentive accurately. The finding is about what the metric rewards, not about anyone's character, and that framing keeps the team willing to help fix it.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What metric set would replace tickets closed? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Outcomes with counter-metrics. First-contact resolution, meaning tickets resolved without being reopened or followed by another contact within seven days; customer satisfaction on resolved tickets; and time to resolution, reported by ticket complexity so that hard tickets are not penalized. A small quality audit of closed tickets is the guardrail. I would set team goals on resolution and satisfaction and use the count of tickets closed only as a workload diagnostic, so that no one is rewarded for closing without solving.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"c05-12-exercises\"></span>\n<h3>12. Exercises</h3>\n<p>Ten exercises. Each has a model solution and an alternative. Figures are assumptions unless marked.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c05-01\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C05-01 · Write metric definition cards</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Write a definition card (FW-C05-01) for each vague metric: (a) &quot;engagement&quot; for a music app, (b) &quot;retention&quot; for a meal-delivery app, (c) &quot;conversion&quot; for an online store.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Three teams use these words in dashboards with different meanings.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None beyond the names.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Each card needs event, numerator, denominator, population, window, aggregation, unit and one caveat.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Choose a defensible definition and write it so that two analysts compute the same number.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> (a) Weekly listening days: the share of weekly active listeners who listened for at least 10 minutes on at least four days in a calendar week; event: playback of 10 minutes or more; population: registered listeners with at least one play that week, excluding test accounts; unit: user; caveat: background play on a paused device can inflate days. (b) Four-week order retention: among customers who placed their first order in week 0, the share who placed another order in weeks 1 to 4 (bounded); unit: customer; caveat: cancelled orders excluded. (c) Purchase conversion: orders placed by unique visitors in a seven-day window divided by unique visitors in the window; population: sessions after bot filtering; caveat: visitors who return after seven days are counted twice.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Provide a second definition for each and say when the second would be better (for example, day-N retention for a daily-habit product).</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A narrower definition is easier to audit and may exclude users you care about.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c05-02\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C05-02 · Three retention numbers from one table</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Ten users have these active days after signup: A: 1, 2, 7; B: 1, 3; C: 2, 7, 8; D: 1; E: 7; F: 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7; G: 3, 6, 9; H: 5; I: 7, 10; J: none. Compute day-7 (exact), bounded (any activity in days 1 to 7) and unbounded (active on day 7 or later) retention.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> A team says &quot;7-day retention is 60%&quot; and another says it is 50%.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As listed.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Show the counts.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Apply each definition to each user.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Day-7 exact: active on day 7: A, C, E, F, I, so 5 of 10 = 50%. Bounded: active on some day from 1 to 7: A, B, C, D, E, F, G, H, I, so 9 of 10 = 90%. Unbounded: active on day 7 or later: A, C, E, F, G (day 9), I, so 6 of 10 = 60%. Both teams are right; they used different definitions (exact and unbounded).</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Add a rolling definition (active in any of days 7 to 13) and note the answer depends on the data window.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Bounded retention looks best and says least; match the definition to the product's natural frequency.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c05-03\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C05-03 · Funnel arithmetic</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Of 10,000 visitors, 6,500 start sign-up, 4,800 verify their email, 3,000 take a first action and 1,200 pay. Compute step conversions, absolute losses, the overall rate, and the step to examine first.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> A subscription product wants to improve paid conversion.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As given.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Check that the product of the step rates equals the overall rate.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Compute and compare both absolute and relative losses.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Step conversions: 6,500 ÷ 10,000 = 65%; 4,800 ÷ 6,500 = 73.8%; 3,000 ÷ 4,800 = 62.5%; 1,200 ÷ 3,000 = 40%. Losses: 3,500; 1,700; 1,800; 1,800. Overall: 1,200 ÷ 10,000 = 12%, and 0.65 × 0.738 × 0.625 × 0.40 = 0.12, so it checks. The largest absolute loss is visit to sign-up start (3,500), and the lowest step rate is first action to paid (40%). The first look depends on cost: the last step is closest to revenue, and recovering a third of the 1,800 lost at the last step (600 users) would raise conversion from 12% to 18%.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Segment each step by source to see if one source drives the loss.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> The biggest absolute loss is not always the cheapest to fix.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c05-04\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C05-04 · A metric tree for a subscription app</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> A subscription app has 200,000 subscribers, 12,000 new subscribers a month and a monthly churn of 4%. Average revenue per subscriber is $9 a month. Build the tree, compute next month's subscribers and revenue, and compare two levers: cutting churn to 3% or raising new subscribers by 2,000.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Planning for the next quarter.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As given.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> One month ahead, no seasonality.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Subscribers next month = subscribers + new − churned. Revenue = subscribers × average revenue.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Churned: 200,000 × 4% = 8,000. Next month: 200,000 + 12,000 − 8,000 = 204,000. Revenue at the start: 200,000 × $9 = $1,800,000; at the end: 204,000 × $9 = $1,836,000. Lever 1: churn at 3% loses 6,000, giving 206,000. Lever 2: 14,000 new at 4% churn gives 200,000 + 14,000 − 8,000 = 206,000. Both add 2,000 subscribers in the first month. But the churn lever compounds: each month the base is larger, and each subscriber stays longer, while the new-subscriber lever does not change how long they stay. Cutting churn by one point is worth about as much as a 17% increase in new subscribers (2,000 ÷ 12,000).</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Add price as a third lever and compare revenue.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Reducing churn takes longer to show up and costs engineering time; acquisition can be bought with spend.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c05-05\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C05-05 · Choose a north star with the five tests</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Test three candidates for a language-learning app and choose one: (a) minutes in app per user, (b) lessons completed per active user per week, (c) weekly active learners who complete at least one lesson.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> The company monetizes through subscriptions.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None beyond the descriptions.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Apply value, control, speed, gaming and clarity.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Use FW-C05-03.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> (a) Value weak (minutes can rise with confusion or idle app), gaming easy (leave it open). Reject. (b) Value medium; it rises when active users do more, but a few heavy users can lift it, and easier lessons raise it. Adapt. (c) Value strong: it counts people who did something that is learning; it reflects reach and completion; control yes; speed weekly; gaming moderate (very short lessons) but a lesson-length floor and a counter metric (assessment pass rate on completed lessons) reduce it; clarity high with a definition of a completed lesson. Adopt (c), with inputs of new learners, retained learners and reactivated learners, and a counter metric on assessment results.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Combine (b) and (c) into a pair: reach and depth, as two primary measures for different teams.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> (c) ignores depth of use; the pair gives depth at the cost of two numbers to explain.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c05-06\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C05-06 · Guardrails and counter metrics</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> For each change, name a primary metric, two guardrails with thresholds, and a counter metric: (a) a faster checkout flow; (b) sending more reminder notifications.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Both changes are going to experiments.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> <em>Invented baselines:</em> checkout conversion 3.0%, refund rate 2.0%, support contacts per 1,000 orders 40; notification opt-out 1.0% per week, 30-day retention 30%.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Thresholds must be specific.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Ask what the change could damage and what a cheap way to hit the target would look like.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> (a) Primary: checkout conversion. Guardrails: refund rate must not exceed 2.2%; support contacts per 1,000 orders must not exceed 44; payment error rate must not rise. Counter metric: the share of orders edited or cancelled within an hour of purchase, which a flow that hides review steps would raise. (b) Primary: orders from reminder recipients. Guardrails: weekly notification opt-out must not exceed 1.3%; 30-day retention must not fall below 29.5%. Counter metric: app uninstalls in the week after a reminder, and the share of reminders that lead to an order within a day (low means they are noise).</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Write the thresholds as the smallest change you would care about, tied to a power calculation (C12).</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Tight thresholds block good launches on noise; loose ones miss damage.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c05-07\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C05-07 · How would each metric be gamed?</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Name a way to game each metric and a counter metric: (a) tickets closed, (b) new signups, (c) app opens, (d) a satisfaction survey score, (e) lines of code written.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> A review of team goals.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> One sentence for each part.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Apply inversion: how would I hit this without delivering value?</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> (a) Close easy tickets, or close without solving. Counter: repeat-contact rate. (b) Remove verification or pay for low-intent signups. Counter: activation or 30-day retention of new signups. (c) Notifications that open the app, or automatic refresh. Counter: sessions with a meaningful action and notification opt-out. (d) Survey only happy customers or ask leading questions. Counter: response rate and a sample survey of all customers. (e) Write verbose or duplicated code. Counter: defects per change and review feedback; better, drop the metric.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Propose a replacement measure for each, and say whether the metric should be dropped.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Every counter metric costs attention; add one that targets the most likely gaming.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c05-08\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C05-08 · A mix shift hidden in an average</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Paid-acquired users retain at 20% at week 4 and organic users at 40%, in every cohort. Before a campaign, 30% of a cohort is paid-acquired; after, 60%. Compute the overall week-4 retention before and after, and explain what happened.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> A dashboard shows overall retention falling and the team suspects the product got worse.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As given.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Show the weighted calculation.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Weight retention by the mix.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Before: 0.3 × 20% + 0.7 × 40% = 6% + 28% = 34%. After: 0.6 × 20% + 0.4 × 40% = 12% + 16% = 28%. Overall retention fell six points even though retention within each source did not change at all; the mix moved toward the lower-retaining source (a <a href=\"#gl-mix-shift\">mix shift</a>, C13). The team should look at retention by source, not at the average, and decide whether the paid users are worth their cost.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Compute retention adjusted to a fixed mix (the before mix) to compare periods fairly.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Splitting by source helps and multiplies the number of figures; split by the dimension most likely to shift.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c05-09\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C05-09 · Write a tracking plan</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Write the tracking plan for a &quot;share a list&quot; feature in a shopping app: events, when they fire, key properties, and the metrics each supports.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> The feature lets a user send a shopping list to a friend, who can open it and add items.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> At least five events; include one server-side event and one data quality check.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Walk the funnel and decide what each metric needs.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Events: list_share_started (client; user opens the share sheet; source screen, list size); list_shared (server; the invitation is created; channel, number of recipients); list_opened (server; a recipient opens it; recipient is existing user or new); list_item_added_by_recipient (server; item added; time since open); list_purchase_completed (server; an order includes items from a shared list). Metrics: share rate (shared ÷ started), open rate (opened ÷ shared), collaboration rate (opened lists with an item added), and new users from shares. Data quality check: opens should never exceed shares plus link forwards; track the ratio daily, and check that events arrive within a minute on both platforms.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Add a consent-aware version: for users who decline analytics, count only server-side aggregates.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Fewer events are easier to maintain; each event should support a metric someone will use.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c05-10\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C05-10 · Eight-minute drill: success metrics</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> In eight minutes, define success for a &quot;reading list&quot; feature in a blogging app.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> A timed spoken drill using the answer template.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None given.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Goal, behavior, primary metric with a definition, inputs, guardrail, counter metric, target and measurement.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Template FW-C05-06.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Goal: readers return to posts they intended to read. Behavior: they read what they saved. Primary metric: the saved-post read rate: saved posts read to 50% within seven days divided by posts saved that week (unit: post; excludes bulk imports). Inputs: share of weekly readers who save, saves per saver, reminder click rate. Guardrails: total reading per reader must not fall; load time flat.</p>\n<p>Counter metric: saved posts never opened in 30 days. Target: 35% read rate in eight weeks. Measurement: events for save, open, and read depth, and a holdout without the save button. Risk: saving is cheap and may substitute for reading now; the read rate and the unread-clutter counter would show it. Decision it drives: invest in reminders if the read rate is low and adoption high.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Answer with the HEART categories first, then choose one metric per category that matters.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> In eight minutes you can define one primary metric well; add inputs briefly and say what you would add with more time.</p>\n<span id=\"c05-13-practice-protocol\"></span>\n<h3>13. Practice protocol</h3>\n<p><strong>Timed drills (three a week for three weeks).</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Week 1: define one metric.</em> Pick a prompt (the exercises and IQ-C05-01 to IQ-C05-08). In five minutes, write the metric definition card (FW-C05-01) for the primary metric and test it against the five tests.</li>\n<li><em>Week 2: the full answer.</em> Use the ten-minute budget in section 4 and the template (FW-C05-06). Speak aloud or record.</li>\n<li><em>Week 3: add data.</em> Ask a partner to give a number partway (&quot;DAU is up 5%&quot;) and to challenge one definition. Update aloud.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Weekly habit.</strong> Pick one product you use. Write its north star, three inputs, one guardrail and one counter metric on a single page. Compare your choices with what the product seems to optimize, using only what you can observe.</p>\n<p><strong>Feedback checklist (yes, partly, no).</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Did I start with the goal and the user behavior?</li>\n<li>Did I choose one primary metric?</li>\n<li>Is the definition complete: event, numerator, denominator, population, window?</li>\n<li>Did I add two or three inputs?</li>\n<li>Did I name guardrails with thresholds?</li>\n<li>Did I name a counter metric for the target?</li>\n<li>Did I say how the metric could be gamed or could mislead?</li>\n<li>Did I state a target and time frame, and distinguish leading from lagging?</li>\n<li>Did I say how I would measure it, and the privacy or measurement bias?</li>\n<li>Did I name the decision the metric drives?</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>With a partner.</strong> Ask your partner to say &quot;how could I hit that number without helping anyone?&quot; and &quot;what does that metric leave out?&quot; once each. Practice answering with a counter metric, not a defense.</p>\n<p><strong>Answer journal.</strong> For each drill record the date, the prompt, the primary metric and its definition, your score out of ten, the item to fix, and the gaming path you missed. After six drills, look for the item you most often skip (usually the counter metric or the definition) and drill that one.</p>\n<span id=\"c05-14-implementation-playbook\"></span>\n<h3>14. Implementation playbook</h3>\n<p>Apply the chapter on real work over the next two weeks. Each deliverable doubles as an interview story.</p>\n<p><strong>Days 1 to 3.</strong> Pick the three metrics your team reviews most often. Write a definition card (FW-C05-01) for each. Check the definition against what the dashboard computes; note every difference, and ask the owner which is intended.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 1.</strong> Run the five tests (FW-C05-03) on your team's north star or primary metric. Write down how it could be gamed and what counter metric you would pair with it. Share the page with your manager and ask whether it matches how the team thinks.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 1.</strong> Do a metrics audit (C05.7) for one dashboard: for each metric, what decision it informs, who owns it, and when it last changed an action. Propose dropping or replacing one metric that fails.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 2.</strong> Write the tracking plan for your next feature before it is built, and review it with engineering and analytics. After launch, check the first day of data against the plan.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 2.</strong> Build one cohort retention table for a recent period and read it with FW-C05-05. Note the plateau, the cohort comparison, and one caveat.</p>\n<p><strong>Deliverables that become stories:</strong> the definition cards (a time you made a number precise), the audit (retiring a vanity metric), and the tracking plan (preventing a data gap). Keep the numbers real.</p>\n<span id=\"c05-15-key-takeaways-and-further-reading\"></span>\n<h3>15. Key takeaways and further reading</h3>\n<span id=\"c05-key-takeaways\"></span>\n<h4>Key takeaways</h4>\n<ol>\n<li>Start from the goal and the user behavior that shows it, choose one primary metric, and define it completely: event, numerator, denominator, population, window.</li>\n<li>Surround the primary metric with inputs that explain it, guardrails with thresholds that protect other things, and a counter metric for the target.</li>\n<li>Ask how any metric could be raised without delivering value, because a measure that becomes a target gets gamed.</li>\n<li>Read retention by cohort, find the plateau, check for mix shift, and treat a correlation with retention as a hypothesis to test.</li>\n<li>Every metric should change a decision; if it never has, retire it.</li>\n</ol>\n<span id=\"c05-further-reading\"></span>\n<h4>Further reading</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Kerry Rodden, Hilary Hutchinson and Xin Fu, &quot;Measuring the User Experience on a Large Scale: User-Centered Metrics for Web Applications&quot; (CHI 2010). The HEART framework and the Goals–Signals–Metrics process <code>[V: Rodden, Hutchinson and Fu, CHI 2010, doi 10.1145/1753326.1753687]</code>.</li>\n<li>Alistair Croll and Benjamin Yoskovitz, <em>Lean Analytics</em> (2013). Choosing a metric for each stage of a startup.</li>\n<li>Eric Ries, <em>The Lean Startup</em> (2011). Actionable metrics versus vanity metrics.</li>\n<li>Ron Kohavi, Diane Tang and Ya Xu, <em>Trustworthy Online Controlled Experiments</em> (2020). Metrics for experiments, including guardrails; developed further in C14.</li>\n<li>Marilyn Strathern, &quot;Improving Ratings: Audit in the British University System&quot; (1997). Source of the common phrasing of Goodhart's law <code>[V: Strathern 1997]</code>.</li>\n<li>Colin Bryar and Bill Carr, <em>Working Backwards</em> (2021). Accounts from former Amazon executives, including input metrics <code>[V: St. Martin's Press, 9 February 2021, ISBN 9781250267597; includes a chapter on managing inputs, not outputs]</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"c06\"></span>\n<h2>C06 — Users, Problems and Discovery</h2>\n<span id=\"c06-1-what-interviewers-test\"></span>\n<h3>1. What interviewers test</h3>\n<p>The skill is understanding people well enough to decide what is worth building: who they are, what they are trying to do, where it goes wrong, and how sure you are. C02 trained you to decide with the cost visible, C03 to structure a question, and C05 to measure. This chapter supplies the inputs that make the rest worth anything, because a well-structured, well-measured decision about the wrong problem is still wrong.</p>\n<p><strong>Where it shows up.</strong> <code>Inferred</code> from the round structures candidates commonly report: the user and pain-point steps of every product design and improvement answer (C07, C08), the &quot;how would you research this?&quot; prompt, behavioral questions about how you learned what users needed, and follow-ups such as &quot;how do you know users want that?&quot; On the job it appears in every conversation that begins with &quot;customers keep asking for...&quot;, in interviews and survey design, and in every spec that has to say why.</p>\n<p><strong>What is scored.</strong> Google has long published a stated philosophy that begins with focusing on the user <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Google, Ten things we know to be true]</code>, and it publicly lists role-related knowledge among its hiring attributes <code>Public</code> <code>[V: summaries citing Google's how-we-hire page; Google re:Work structured-interviewing guide]</code>. Amazon publishes Customer Obsession as a leadership principle <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Amazon's Leadership Principles page, amazon.jobs]</code>. Microsoft has publicly described being customer-obsessed as part of its culture <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Microsoft careers culture page]</code>. In practice, interviewers listen for whether your user is specific, whether your evidence is behavior rather than opinion, and whether you can say what research can and cannot tell you <code>Inferred</code>. Five observable behaviors:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>You separate the problem from the solution and from the feature.</li>\n<li>You segment users by need and behavior and choose a segment by a stated criterion.</li>\n<li>You choose a research method to fit the question, and name its biases.</li>\n<li>You write the problem so that a team could act on it, without naming the solution.</li>\n<li>You design the cheapest test of your riskiest assumption and say what would change your mind.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>The failure this prevents.</strong> Five patterns cost candidates and new PMs: demographic users (&quot;college students&quot;), taking requests at face value, trusting what people say they would do, a research plan that is only &quot;run a survey,&quot; and discovery that never ends in a decision.</p>\n<span id=\"c06-2-the-concept-from-first-principles\"></span>\n<h3>2. The concept from first principles</h3>\n<span id=\"c06-c06-1-what-a-user-problem-is-problem-versus-solution-versus-\"></span>\n<h4>C06.1 What a user problem is: problem versus solution versus feature</h4>\n<p>A <strong>user problem</strong> is an unmet need, friction or goal of a specific person in a specific situation. It has three layers that people constantly confuse (FW-C06-01):</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Layer</th>\n<th>What it is</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Problem</td>\n<td>The need or friction</td>\n<td>&quot;I can't find a file I saved last week&quot;</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Solution</td>\n<td>A way to meet it</td>\n<td>Search</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Feature</td>\n<td>A specific implementation</td>\n<td>A search bar with filters and recent items</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Requests almost always arrive as features (&quot;add a filter,&quot; &quot;give us a dashboard,&quot; &quot;we need an app&quot;). The PM's first move is to step up the ladder: what is this person trying to do, what is in the way, and what are they doing about it today? <a href=\"#gl-product-thinking\">product thinking</a> from C02 applies directly: solutions are cheap to generate and expensive to build, and the problem is what lets you choose among them.</p>\n<p><strong>Stepping up the ladder.</strong> Ask: &quot;What are you trying to get done?&quot; &quot;What happens when you try?&quot; &quot;What do you do instead today?&quot; &quot;What would change if it were solved?&quot; The <a href=\"#gl-five-whys\">five whys</a> (C03) is a useful discipline, with a caution: asked as an interrogation it feels like one, so phrase it as curiosity about the last time it happened.</p>\n<p><strong>Why it works.</strong> The problem constrains the solution space and survives changes in technology, while a feature request reflects the requester's current imagination and tools. Two different requests (&quot;add a filter,&quot; &quot;let me save searches&quot;) may turn out to be the same problem, and two identical requests may be different problems.</p>\n<p><strong>When not to use it.</strong> When the request is a plain defect, or the solution is obvious, cheap and reversible, spending a week to find the &quot;real problem&quot; is waste (<a href=\"#gl-reversible-decision\">reversible decision</a>, C02). Power users who ask for a keyboard shortcut usually know exactly what they want.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Counterexample.</strong> A team treats every request as a symptom of a deeper need and builds elaborate discovery for each. A customer asks for a column in an export. The need is that the column is missing; there is no deeper job. Stepping up the ladder is a tool for requests that could be met in several ways. When one way is clearly the answer, take it and move on.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Hidden risk.</strong> Reframing can become a way to avoid saying no politely, or to impose your favorite solution. Check the reframed problem with the requester: &quot;Is that what you are trying to do?&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"c06-c06-2-segmenting-users-by-need-behavior-and-context\"></span>\n<h4>C06.2 Segmenting users by need, behavior and context</h4>\n<p>A <strong>user segment</strong> is a group of users defined by shared needs or behavior that matter to the decision at hand. <a href=\"#gl-user-segment\">user segment</a> is the unit of every product answer, because &quot;users&quot; is rarely one group. A product for &quot;everyone&quot; is usually good for no one in particular.</p>\n<p><strong>Cut by need, behavior and context, not by demographics.</strong> &quot;Women aged 25 to 34&quot; says nothing about what they want. &quot;Weekly commuters who change routes often&quot; does. Demographics are useful only when they correlate with a need (language, accessibility, regulatory status, household size for a family product).</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Basis</th>\n<th>Example segments</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Need or job</td>\n<td>People planning a trip; people dealing with a delay</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Behavior</td>\n<td>Daily users; occasional users; lapsed users</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Context</td>\n<td>On a phone with a poor connection; at a desk; in a car</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Experience</td>\n<td>First-time; expert</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Stakes</td>\n<td>Casual; professional or high-stakes</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Choosing a segment.</strong> FW-C06-02 gives a scorecard. Four criteria cover most decisions: the size of the segment, the intensity of its pain, whether you can reach it, and whether you have a reason to win with it (strategic fit and capability). Say the criterion out loud, and say what you give up.</p>\n<p><strong>Extreme users.</strong> People at the edges (a heavy user, a person with a disability, someone with a poor connection) often reveal needs that average users share more mildly. Studying them is a design practice associated with IDEO <code>[V: IDEO.org, The Field Guide to Human-Centered Design, 2015: method Extremes and Mainstreams; IDEO Design Kit]</code>, and it is a cheap way to see a problem sharply.</p>\n<p><strong>Why it works.</strong> A narrow, well-understood segment gives a team something to design for and to measure. It also gives you a hypothesis to test: do this segment's behaviors differ in the way I think?</p>\n<p><strong>When not to use it.</strong> When the need really is universal and the segments differ only in degree (a payment confirmation message), segmentation adds ceremony.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Counterexample.</strong> Demographic segmentation does the right job when need follows demographics. A banking app for people who do not read the local language is a demographic-defined problem (language). Accessibility for blind users is defined by a condition. Segment by need and behavior as the default, and by demographic when the need requires it.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Hidden risk.</strong> Segments that are too narrow to matter or too broad to act on. Test: could you name five real people in it, and could a team design differently for them?</p>\n<span id=\"c06-c06-3-jobs-to-be-done\"></span>\n<h4>C06.3 Jobs to be done</h4>\n<p><strong>Jobs to be done</strong> is a lens that describes the progress a user is trying to make in a situation, which the product is hired to help with. <a href=\"#gl-jobs-to-be-done\">jobs to be done</a> shifts attention from who the user is to what they are trying to accomplish, and from the product to the alternatives they would use instead. It is associated with Clayton Christensen's work and with Anthony Ulwick's outcome-driven approach <code>[V: Christensen, Hall, Dillon and Duncan, Harvard Business Review, September 2016; Ulwick's firm, Strategyn, on outcome-driven innovation]</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>The job statement.</strong> A common format: &quot;When [situation], I want to [motivation], so I can [expected outcome].&quot; For example: &quot;When I'm on a long train ride with no signal, I want something to listen to that I picked earlier, so I can pass the time without stress.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Three dimensions.</strong> A job has a functional side (get from A to B), an emotional side (feel in control) and a social side (look prepared in front of a manager). Products often win or lose on the emotional and social sides.</p>\n<p><strong>Competing solutions.</strong> The alternatives to a product are whatever else gets the job done, including doing nothing and workarounds. A spreadsheet is a competitor to project-management software, and a long walk may be a competitor to a taxi.</p>\n<p><strong>An often-cited illustration.</strong> Christensen and colleagues have described a fast-food chain's milkshake that was &quot;hired&quot; for a long, boring morning commute, a job different from the one a dessert milkshake does (published accounts include Christensen, Cook and Hall, &quot;Marketing Malpractice,&quot; <em>Harvard Business Review</em>, December 2005, and the book <em>Competing Against Luck</em>, 2016 <code>[V: HBS Working Knowledge, &quot;Clay Christensen's Milkshake Marketing&quot;; Competing Against Luck excerpt; HBS case note citing the 2005 HBR article]</code>). The story is a reminder that the same product can be hired for different jobs, which changes what a better product looks like.</p>\n<p><strong>Why it works.</strong> Jobs are stable over time while solutions change, and they surface competition and context that segment descriptions miss.</p>\n<p><strong>When not to use it.</strong> When the job is trivially obvious or too abstract to guide a decision. &quot;Help people be happy&quot; is a job statement that tells a team nothing.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Counterexample.</strong> A team writes the job for a note-taking app as &quot;help people be productive.&quot; Every feature fits and no decision follows. A sharper statement (&quot;when I leave a meeting, I want to capture the three decisions and who owns them, so I can follow up without rereading everything&quot;) rules features in and out. If a job statement would justify any feature, it is too abstract.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Hidden risk.</strong> Jobs written from the desk are guesses. A job statement is a hypothesis until interviews or observation support it.</p>\n<span id=\"c06-c06-4-personas-and-journey-maps-use-and-misuse\"></span>\n<h4>C06.4 Personas and journey maps: use and misuse</h4>\n<p>A <strong>persona</strong> is a composite profile of a user type used to align a team. <a href=\"#gl-persona\">persona</a>s help when a team disagrees about who the user is, and when each persona is built from research and ties to behaviors and decisions. They fail when they are invented in a workshop, decorated with a stock photo and a hobby, and then ignored, or when they encode stereotypes. A &quot;proto-persona&quot; written from assumptions is acceptable only if labeled as a hypothesis to test.</p>\n<p>A <strong>journey map</strong> lays out a user's path to an outcome in stages, with what they do, think and feel, and where the friction is, so a team can see moments of pain and opportunity. Build it from observation or interviews. Mark what is known and what is guessed.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Stage</th>\n<th>Awareness</th>\n<th>Consideration</th>\n<th>First use</th>\n<th>Ongoing use</th>\n<th>Problem</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Actions</td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Thoughts</td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Emotion (high, low)</td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Pain points</td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Opportunities</td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Why they work.</strong> They make a shared picture of a person or path visible, so a team argues about the same thing.</p>\n<p><strong>When not to use them.</strong> When the team already shares a clear picture, or when producing the artifact substitutes for talking to users. A pretty map with no research behind it is decoration.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Counterexample.</strong> A team spends three weeks creating polished personas for a feature used by 40 customers it knows by name. The personas add nothing the team does not already know. Personas earn their cost when many people must act on a shared understanding of users they cannot all meet.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Hidden risk.</strong> An artifact can freeze an early guess. Update personas and maps when research changes them, and date them.</p>\n<span id=\"c06-c06-5-qualitative-research-interviews-and-observation\"></span>\n<h4>C06.5 Qualitative research: interviews and observation</h4>\n<p>Qualitative research answers &quot;what is going on and why?&quot; in depth, with a small number of people. It generates hypotheses; it does not measure how common something is.</p>\n<p><strong>Interviews.</strong> FW-C06-04 gives a guide. The rules that matter most:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Ask about the past, not the future.</strong> &quot;Tell me about the last time you planned a trip&quot; produces evidence. &quot;Would you use an app that planned trips?&quot; produces a polite guess. This is the core idea of Rob Fitzpatrick's <em>The Mom Test</em> (2013) <code>[V: CreateSpace Independent Publishing Platform, 10 September 2013, ISBN 9781492180746]</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Ask open questions, and do not lead.</strong> &quot;What did you do next?&quot; not &quot;Was that frustrating?&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Follow the specifics.</strong> &quot;Show me.&quot; &quot;What happened then?&quot; &quot;Why was that a problem?&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Look for workarounds.</strong> What people build for themselves (a spreadsheet, a sticky note, a group chat) is evidence of a real problem that they care about enough to fix.</li>\n<li><strong>Listen more than you talk,</strong> and tolerate silence.</li>\n<li><strong>Do not sell.</strong> If you pitch your idea, you will hear polite agreement.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Observation.</strong> Watching people do the task, in their own environment, shows what they do not say: steps they skip, tools they use, workarounds they have stopped noticing. Contextual inquiry, shadowing, diary studies and reading support tickets are all forms of it.</p>\n<p><strong>A well-known illustration.</strong> Airbnb's founders are widely reported to have gone through New York listings with Paul Graham in 2009, seen that the photos were poor, flown to New York and replaced the photos themselves, after which bookings rose (accounts differ on the size of the rise) <code>[V: First Round Review account of Airbnb's early growth, as quoted; Paul Graham's essay on doing things that don't scale was not opened]</code>. The lesson commonly drawn is that looking closely at real listings, and then fixing the problem by hand before building anything scalable, revealed a problem that aggregate metrics had not.</p>\n<p><strong>How many people.</strong> For finding usability problems with a design, a small number of testers finds most of the repeated problems (a rule of thumb from Jakob Nielsen's work <code>[V: Nielsen and Landauer, 1993; Nielsen, Why You Only Need to Test with 5 Users (2000): about 85% of problems]</code>). For understanding needs across several segments, plan more: enough in each segment that new interviews stop producing new themes (saturation). Six to eight interviews per segment is a common starting point, not a rule <code>Inferred</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Analysis.</strong> Write each observation on its own note, group similar notes (affinity mapping), name each group, and count how many participants support each. Distinguish what people did from what they said.</p>\n<p><strong>Biases.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Confirmation:</em> hearing what you hoped. Defense: write the hypotheses you would reject before you start.</li>\n<li><em>Social desirability:</em> people say what sounds good. Defense: ask about specific past events.</li>\n<li><em>Interviewer effects:</em> your tone and questions shape answers. Defense: script open questions, review recordings.</li>\n<li><em>Sampling:</em> the people who agree to talk differ from those who do not. Defense: recruit for the segment, including lapsed users.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>When not to use it.</strong> To size anything. Five interviews cannot tell you how many users share a problem.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Counterexample.</strong> A team interviews eight enthusiastic customers and concludes &quot;users want custom reports.&quot; The eight were recruited through the account manager who sells reports. They are not a sample of the segment. Qualitative research finds what to look at; it needs a representative recruit and, to size the finding, quantitative evidence.</p>\n</blockquote>\n";
